-- No CREATE TABLE here — this file exists only to hold cross-table reads that do not belong to any
-- single table's own `.sq` file. See accounts.sq / categories.sq / transactions.sq /
-- recurring_movements.sq for the schema.

-- The dirty-flag watermark: the latest `updatedAt` across all four backed-up tables, in ONE
-- statement.
--
-- Deliberately counts soft-deleted rows. `softDelete` (see e.g. accounts.sq:softDelete) bumps
-- `updatedAt` when it sets `deletedAt`, so a deletion IS a local change and must be able to trigger
-- a backup. This is the OPPOSITE of every `all:`/export read in this module, which filters
-- `deletedAt IS NULL` — those describe what the device currently HOLDS; this describes whether the
-- device has changed AT ALL since the last backup. Do NOT add a `deletedAt IS NULL` filter here to
-- "match" the export: a device whose only change since the last backup was a deletion would then
-- never mark itself dirty, and the deleted row would survive in the last snapshot forever.
--
-- ONE statement, not four reads. Four independent `SELECT max(updatedAt)` calls could interleave
-- with a write landing between any two of them and produce a watermark that is not any state the
-- database was ever actually in — the same race Phase 2a closed on the export itself by moving it
-- inside a single `transactionWithResult`. A `UNION ALL` of the four per-table maxima, reduced by
-- one outer `max`, is a single read and cannot straddle a write.
--
-- Each per-table `SELECT max(updatedAt)` always returns exactly one row — an aggregate with no
-- GROUP BY does, even over zero rows, where it returns NULL — so the UNION ALL always yields four
-- rows and the outer `max` always yields exactly one. SQLite's `max()` ignores NULL inputs and
-- only returns NULL when every input is NULL, so the result is NULL precisely when all four tables
-- hold no rows at all — no local data ever written — and that NULL must stay distinguishable from
-- a real watermark of epoch 0.
latestLocalChange:
SELECT max(m) AS updatedAt FROM (
    SELECT max(updatedAt) AS m FROM accounts
    UNION ALL
    SELECT max(updatedAt) AS m FROM categories
    UNION ALL
    SELECT max(updatedAt) AS m FROM transactions
    UNION ALL
    SELECT max(updatedAt) AS m FROM recurring_movements
);
